Results of the scripted players (more matches may be appended in `playtest/shots/*-log.json`; "hits" = total paddle hits in the match, not one rally):
- idle: lost 0–5 in 24 s.
- casual: lost 1–5 (50 s, 18 hits), 0–5 (37 s, 13 hits), 2–5 (58 s, 21 hits); 0–5 (62 s, 28 hits). Final casual log: 4 matches, 4 computer wins, Esc on the end screen → title confirmed, no errors (`shots/casual-log.json`).
- good: won all 4 matches 5–0 (138 s, 145 s, 140 s, 81 s); no errors (`shots/good-log.json`).

## How it plays (judgement, with the caveat above)
- **Difficulty:** the computer is not trivial — a sloppy follower loses every match, and even a bot with a modest 2/5 of the points never won. An attentive anticipating player beat it 5–0 without the computer scoring once, so a skilled human will probably win clearly; the computer's only weakness is that it sometimes aims off-centre and misses on long rallies (first point of that match fell at 4 s, the others 11–40 s apart). The brief wants "the computer also wins points": it does against sloppy play, but against a good player it won no points in 4 of 4 matches. Not a defect; a note for tuning if the producer wants closer matches.
- **Clarity:** title tells the controls and the goal; end screen names the winner and how to play again; score always visible. The serve pause is long enough to follow. First point of the first rally arrives within ~2–4 s of "Get ready!".
- **Minor:** (1) on the title screen the attract-mode ball can sit over the subtitle text, and the "Press Enter or Space" prompt is absent half of every second — cosmetic. (2) The pause screen advertises Q but the end screen advertises Esc for title; different keys for the same "go to title" idea (cosmetic). (3) The window losing focus pauses a running match (intended, but a player who alt-tabs back sees PAUSED with no hint beyond the on-screen text).
- Sound: could not be heard in headless mode; I only confirm `M` toggles the "sound on/off" label and that the audio files load with no errors.

## Verdict
**Playable and complete: R1–R9 all observed working on the delivered files, with no crash, stuck ball or console error.** I found no blocking defect. Open cosmetic and tuning notes are above.

## Not covered
Real hands-on play by a human, audio, browsers other than Chromium, touch/mouse (click on title/end screen starts a match per code; not tested by me), very long sessions.
